Certificate Authentication (DROPBEAR_CERT_KEYS) - Known Limitations & Future Work
==================================================================================

1. No Revocation Support
   There is no implementation for a RevokedKeys list (similar to OpenSSH's KRL).
   If a CA key is compromised or a certificate needs early revocation, there is
   no built-in mechanism to block it other than removing the CA from
   authorized_keys. An initial step could be a simple serial/key-id blacklist
   file; full KRL binary format support would be a larger effort.

2. Refactoring checkpubkey_cert()
   The checkpubkey_cert() function in src/svr-authpubkey.c is quite large and
   performs complex manual parsing of the authorized_keys line (cert-authority
   detection, quote-aware option tokenization, CA key matching, cert validation).
   This could be refactored into smaller helper functions for better
   maintainability.

3. Test Coverage
   End-to-end tests exist in test_e2e.sh covering the main cert auth paths
   (ed25519, RSA with sha256/sha512 CA sigs, quoted-comma options, negative
   cases, dbclient agent interop). Still missing:
   - Unit tests for cert_parse() with malformed/truncated blobs
   - Fuzzer harness for certificate blob parsing
   - Tests for expired/not-yet-valid certificates
   - Tests for principal mismatch rejection
   - Tests for critical options handling
